abstract: >-
  The widespread deployment of Large Language Models via closed application
  programming interfaces (APIs) in high-stakes domains has made interpretability
  indispensable. This thesis introduces ESCI (Energy-Based Surrogate for
  Concept-Level Interpretation), a post-hoc framework based on amortized
  inference. ESCI elevates the unit of analysis from tokens to complete
  sentences to prevent syntactic breakdown and out-of-distribution errors.
  It trains an Energy-Based Model (EBM) as an unnormalized compatibility
  surrogate, which guides a lightweight interpreter network to perform
  sentence-level feature attribution in constant O(1) time without querying
  the target black-box model at test time.
keywords:
  - "Black-box Large Language Models"
  - "Post-hoc Interpretability"
  - "Sentence-level Feature Attribution"
  - "Energy-Based Models"
  - "Amortized Inference"
  - "Generative AI Auditing"
